      <h2>Why credit prices the bond</h2>
      <p>A surety bond creates a repayment obligation. If the surety pays a valid claim, the principal reimburses that payment, so the rate follows credit.</p>

      <h2>Rate ranges this sketch uses</h2>
      <p>These are the bands the premium sketch on this site uses for a standard license or permit bond. A licensed desk sets the rate on the file.</p>
      <p><strong>Excellent (720+):</strong> 1–3% of the bond amount. A $25,000 face is $250–$750 a year on this band.</p>
      <p><strong>Good (680–719):</strong> 2–5%. A $25,000 face is $500–$1,250 a year on this band.</p>
      <p><strong>Fair (600–679):</strong> 5–8%. A $25,000 face is $1,250–$2,000 a year on this band.</p>
      <p><strong>Below 600:</strong> 8–15%. A $25,000 face is $2,000–$3,750 a year on this band. Some forms also ask for collateral.</p>

      <h2>What the application does to a score</h2>
      <p>A standard license-bond quote on this path uses a soft pull. A soft pull does not change the credit score, so the quote itself is not a new hard inquiry.</p>

      <h2>What a lower score changes</h2>
      <p>A lower score raises the rate inside the bands above. The same form can still be written, sometimes with collateral, through a surety that takes that file.</p>
      <p>The practical path is the <a href="/agencies/integrity-first-insurance/">Integrity First Insurance</a> desk, which can take the form to more than one market.</p>

      <h2>What else the surety reads</h2>
      <p>On a small license bond, credit often sets the rate. On a larger contract bond, the surety also reads financial statements, experience, prior claims, and the bond amount.</p>

      <h2>How to land in a lower band</h2>
      <p>Check the score before you apply. A score sitting just under 720 or 680 can move a file into the next band on this sketch once revolving balances come down and report errors are corrected.</p>
      <p>Use the <a href="/#calculator">premium sketch</a>, then call <a href="/agencies/integrity-first-insurance/">Integrity First Insurance</a>.</p>
    </div>
